cargo-deny for PexOS
====================

Vendored dependencies
---------------------

The crates.io dependencies are vendored in vendor/ and the build uses them
via .cargo/config.toml. After importing a new upstream version (or changing
Cargo.lock), refresh them with:

    cargo vendor --locked vendor

Some vendored crates are patched to build with the Rust version in PexOS;
see the git history for "patch vendored" commits, and update the patched
files' entries in the crate's .cargo-checksum.json.

debian/copyright
----------------

The vendor/ stanzas of debian/copyright are generated; edit
debian/copyright.in (header, upstream and per-file exceptions) instead.
Regenerate after any change to vendor/:

    cargo install --locked --features cli cargo-about
    cargo about generate --frozen --all-features --fail --format json \
        -c debian/about.toml -o /tmp/about.json
    debian/gen-copyright /tmp/about.json > debian/copyright

cargo-about resolves and verifies the licences of the crates used on Linux
(see debian/about.toml); --fail stops if a licence is unknown or not in the
accepted list. Crates only used on other platforms take their licence from
Cargo.toml. gen-copyright fails if it meets a licence it has no Debian name
or licence text for.

cargo-about only looks at each crate's declared licence and licence files,
so check for embedded code under other licences (e.g. bundled C sources) and
record it in debian/copyright.in. The licenserecon package helps:

    lrc

Expected lrc differences are licence texts used as data or test fixtures
(vendor/spdx/src/text/, examples/14_license_detection/), Unicode-3.0 being
reported as Unicode-DFS-2016, and target-lexicon's Apache-2.0 with LLVM
exception licence file being reported as Apache-2.0.
